        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
The objective of this proposal is to use suborbital spaceflights in SpaceShipTwo to develop an understanding of the genomic and metabolic plant responses upon entry into spaceflight. The long term goal of this line of research is a comprehensive appreciation of the biological responses that occur as terrestrial life experiences phases and aspects of spaceflight. We will build on our previous genomic work using Arabidopsis plants by imaging green fluorescent protein (GFP)-tagged promoters from specific genes indicated as potentially involved in early spaceflight transitions and by fixing plants during suborbital flight for whole genome expression analysis with arrays and RNASeq.
<P>
APPROACH:<br>
This project makes extensive use of a unique middeck locker imager system that was developed within the NASA Flight Opportunities Program, an imager that is capable of delivering multispectral fluorescent images at high resolution within space vehicles. The project also utilizes the specialized experience of the Principal Investigator (PI) team in deploying plant molecular biology gene expression experiments and imaging in diverse space-related environments. These studies are innovative in that they will provide the first molecular genetic data from suborbital spaceflights, thereby filling a substantial gap in knowledge regarding the early stages and temporal dynamics of gene expression responses to spaceflight. Specifically these data will provide insights into the effects of early stage spaceflight responses so as to begin to round out a more comprehensive understanding of the temporal and transitional plant responses to spaceflight.
<P>
RESULTS:<br>
In June of 2015 the FLEX imager was flown on a parabolic flight campaign (53-SP Gene Expression). Since this time, we have used the data and information collected to continue development of the imaging system and our understanding of what may be expected of early spaceflight response in plants. The imaging system data was paired with plants fixed during early parabolic flight to help us with future selection of early responding genes to consider as experimental candidates for upcoming sub-orbital flights.
There were two associated objectives of the 2015 parabolic campaign:

First, to further develop the genome level, genetic analyses initiated with earlier parabolic flight and orbital experiments. Numerous genes associated with auxin metabolism are up-regulated in response to both parabolic flight and spaceflight. The genetic analyses in the parabolic flight will contribute to the further dissection of this response.

In preparation for future sub-orbital flights work is being completed to understand molecular response in early flight. In order to study early reactions to flight, plants were loaded in to KFTs prior to take-off and were fixed in rapid succession during the first maneuvers of parabolic flight. This rapid succession was an attempt to capture gene expression patterns induced by early responses to gravitational changes RNA extracted from these plants has been extracted and analyzed using microarrays to determine gene expression levels. Genes that are identified to be quick responding genes will be used for possible future production of transgenic plants to facilitate understanding of the genes localization, pathways, and the roles they play in a plants response to early flight.

Second, we obtained a replicate data-set characterizing the extent of thermal variation on the surface of Arabidopsis leaves during parabolic flight. Previous flights were very successful in this endeavor; our experiments demonstrated that if air is not allowed to circulate that the surface leaf temperature can vary by 2-4°C between the 0g and 2g segments of the parabola. It was our goal to repeat these experiments using the next generation FLIR thermal imaging devices in conjunction with plants engineered with gene reporters designed to reveal changes in gene expression in response to heat-induced stress.

The FLEX imaging system is also equipped with FLIR (Forward Looking Infrared) technology that allows visualization of thermal variation of plants (or a subject located in the imager). Thermal responses of plants change across time during flight. Plants that have the ability to rapidly cool themselves early in flight see a reduction in cooling ability later in flight. During parabolic flight wild-type Arabidopsis was compared to a drought resistant mutant.
        </div>

